@page "/empty-state"

<CatHead Title="Empty and live states">
    What a view shows when it has nothing to show — and what it shows while it is waiting, streaming
    or disconnected. Say which of those it is: they call for different actions, and showing the
    wrong one is how a broken page looks fine.
</CatHead>

<h2 id="state-art">The drawing says which state it is</h2>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.EmptyState.StateArt" Title="State art" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        Thirteen drawings, one per state, in a sprite that ships with the package. A drawing is
        referenced by its id and nothing else &mdash; see the snippet below.
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>One ornament repeated on every empty state says only that the block is styled.</strong>
        The reader still has to read the sentence to learn whether there is no data, no permission or
        no connection — and those need different actions. The drawing is the part that can be read
        first, so it has to differ.
    </p>
    <p>
        Pick by what happened, not by what looks right. <code>#nothing-yet</code> and
        <code>#all-done</code> are both an empty list and are opposites; <code>#failed</code> came
        back wrong and <code>#offline</code> never left; <code>#no-results</code> searched everything
        and <code>#filtered-out</code> excluded it.
    </p>
    <p>
        The sprite writes no colour. Its line work is <code>currentColor</code> and its one accent is
        <code>--state-accent</code>, so <code>.empty-state--failed</code> moves the accent into the
        danger ramp with one declaration and the drawing follows. Inherited custom properties cross
        into the <code>&lt;use&gt;</code> shadow tree, which is what makes that work with no second
        file to keep in step.
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>It is not a second icon set.</strong> An icon set is a vocabulary a page draws from;
        this is thirteen fixed pictures of thirteen fixed states, and
        <a href="/concepts">Remix Icon stays the only icon set</a>.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatSnippet Name="EmptyState/StateArtUse.html" Title="Referencing a drawing">
    <p>
        The path is the package's own static web asset, so it resolves the same in every app and
        nothing is fetched from a remote host. <code>aria-hidden</code> because the drawing repeats
        what the title says: a screen reader announcing it twice is worse than not at all.
    </p>
</CatSnippet>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.EmptyState.StateArtSizes" Title="Three sizes" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        The same state appears in three places and has to fit all of them.
        <code>.state-art--sm</code> is 56px, for a row inside a table or a narrow panel where
        anything larger pushes the message out of view. The bare <code>.state-art</code> is 96px, the
        card size. <code>.state-art--lg</code> is 152px, for a page that is nothing but this — see
        <a href="/common-pages">Common pages</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>There is no step below 56px.</strong> The stroke fills in and the drawing reads as a
        smudge, which is the same reason the brand mark is drawn three times rather than scaled. Where
        there is less room than that, use the sentence on its own.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.EmptyState.EmptyState" Title="Empty" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        Five kinds of empty, and they are not interchangeable. Nothing yet is an invitation.
        Nothing found needs the query echoed back so the reader can see what was searched. Filtered
        to nothing needs the filters named and a way to clear them. No access needs to say who can
        see it and how to ask. And a cleared queue has to look like success, because a finished
        list and a broken one are the same empty box until the drawing says otherwise.
    </p>
    <p>
        Give the one action that resolves it, and no others. Inside a <code>.card</code> for a panel,
        or <code>.empty-state--bare</code> straight on the page.
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>The action is a <code>.btn-sm</code>.</strong> It is the only control in the block
        and it does not need to shout; a default or large button beside a 13px sentence is the
        thing that makes an empty panel look unfinished.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.EmptyState.Pending" Title="Waiting, and failed" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.empty-state--pending</code> is a wait with nothing to report: the first event of a
        stream, a long request. Put a <code>.spinner-lg</code> where the drawing goes — it is the one
        state a still picture cannot carry, because the thing being said is that something is still
        happening. Where the wait is a queue the reader can be told about and then leave, that is
        <code>#waiting</code> and it is a drawing. Where there is
        progress use a <a href="/progress"><code>.progress</code></a> bar; where the shape of what is
        coming is known, use a <code>.skeleton</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
        <code>.empty-state--failed</code> moves <code>--state-accent</code> into the danger ramp and
        leaves the line work grey. A whole red panel reads as destructive — as though something was
        lost — rather than as unavailable. <code>.empty-state-detail</code> is the line that makes a
        failure actionable: the endpoint, the code, the time it was tried, where somebody can read
        it, rather than in a console nobody has open.
    </p>
    <p>
        <code>#failed</code> and <code>#offline</code> are two states, not one.
        <strong>A request that came back wrong and a request that never left need different
        actions</strong>, and only the second one is worth retrying immediately.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.EmptyState.Connection" Title="Connection state" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.health-badge</code> with a <code>.health-dot</code>, in six states. The three active
        ones — <code>--live</code>, <code>--streaming</code>, <code>--connecting</code> — pulse; the
        three settled ones — <code>--idle</code>, <code>--degraded</code>, <code>--down</code> — do
        not.
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>The word is not optional.</strong> A colour alone fails for anybody who cannot see
        it, and green does not say whether it means connected, healthy or finished. Under
        <code>prefers-reduced-motion</code> the pulse stops and the dot keeps its colour.
    </p>
    <p>
        <code>.stale</code> is how old the number beside it is, for anything polled or pushed.
        <code>--warn</code> when it is old enough to distrust; what counts as old is the app's
        judgement.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.EmptyState.OutputPane" Title="Output pane" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.output</code> is lines of machine output: a build log, a command's stdout, an event
        stream, an audit trail. A real list, so the count is announced and a screen reader can walk
        it. Monospace with tabular figures, because the reader is comparing columns down the page
        rather than reading prose.
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>Add <code>data-follow</code> and it sticks to the newest line.</strong> It releases
        the moment the reader scrolls up to read something, and re-attaches when they scroll back
        down. Without the release, reading anything in a live stream is impossible; without the
        re-attach, it never resumes. <code>sednaUi.output.follow(pane)</code> re-attaches it from
        a &ldquo;jump to latest&rdquo; button.
    </p>
    <p>
        <code>.output-head</code> sits outside the pane so it does not scroll away with the lines.
        Severity tints the whole line rather than marking its start: the reader is scanning a wall of
        text, and a single coloured word is easy to miss in one.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.EmptyState.ActivityRow" Title="Work in progress" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        Background work the reader can see and cancel. There is no activity-row class: it is a
        <a href="/collections"><code>.list-row</code></a> with a
        <a href="/progress"><code>.progress</code></a> bar in its subtext and the controls in
        <code>.list-meta</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
        A determinate bar for work with a known size, an indeterminate one for work that has been
        accepted but not started. Give the cancel button the job's name — a page of buttons all
        labelled &ldquo;Cancel&rdquo; is a page where cancelling is a guess.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<h2 id="page-level">When the whole page is the state</h2>

<p>
    <code>.empty-state--page</code> is the same block at page scale: a bigger disc, a heading
    instead of a strong line, and a wider measure. It is the page-level sibling, not a second
    component &mdash; an access-denied page is a view with nothing in it for this reader, and the
    icon, the sentence and the one action are the same three parts.
</p>

<p>
    Three classes come with it. <code>.empty-state-code</code> is the status code above the title,
    small and monospace, because what the reader needs it for is telling somebody else which page
    broke &mdash; not a numeral the height of the viewport.
    <code>.empty-state-actions</code> is the wrapped, centred row for one primary action and the
    one or two beside it; it also takes the top margin off the buttons, which is right for a single
    action and staggers the moment there are two. The drawing goes up to
    <code>.state-art--lg</code>.
</p>

<p>
    <a href="/common-pages">Common pages</a> has the five finished ones &mdash; access denied, not
    found, the error, the expired session, the maintenance window &mdash; with the shell each
    belongs in.
</p>
